NAME
    shell - the Y1/OS command line: < > >> and | pipes

SYNOPSIS
    cmd [args] [< in] [> out | >> out] [| cmd [args] ...]

DESCRIPTION
    The shell reads a line, splits it at | into up to 4 commands and
    runs them one after the other. Each command is a built-in (cd,
    type, load, run, save, ren, mkdir, rmdir, exit...) or a program:
    /BIN/NAME first, then NAME in the current directory.

        cmd > F      the output goes to the file F; a file F is
                     replaced when cmd is done (until then the old F
                     is still there to read: sort F > F works)
        cmd >> F     the output is added to the end of F (a new F if
                     there is none)
        cmd < F      the input comes from F: what a filter reads when
                     no file is named, "-", and conin()
        a | b        a's output is b's input; a | b | c | d at most
        > F          alone: F becomes an empty file

    The clauses come after the arguments (echo hi > F, not echo > F
    hi), in any order, each once per command, with or without a space
    after the operator. | < > inside '...' or "..." are text, so awk
    and echo can use them. Relative names resolve in the directory
    that is current when that command starts.

    Redirection works for the built-ins too (type F > G). The prompt,
    the shell's own messages ("what?", "syntax: ...", "cannot read the
    < file", "cannot write the > or pipe file", "not found") and every
    command's error messages go to the screen, never into a file or a
    pipe. Keys go to the keyboard: more, man and md page a pipe while
    their --More-- key comes from the keyboard, and they do not page
    at all when their own output is a file or a pipe.

HOW PIPES RUN
    There is no multitasking. a | b runs a with its output to the file
    /PIPE0.TMP, then b with its input from it; a third command reads
    /PIPE1.TMP, a fourth /PIPE0.TMP again. The temp files are deleted
    when the line is done; their sectors come back only with a PACK
    (not written yet), like those of every deleted file.

LIMITS
    One file open for writing at a time: while a command's output goes
    to a file (> >> or a pipe to the next command) the command cannot
    create one of its own. cp, mv to another directory, touch, save,
    mkdir, cp -r and vi's :w fail with their "cannot create" message
    there; with < alone they work.
    A redirect holds one of the four file handles (two with < and >)
    while the command runs.
    There is no 2> (errors always go to the screen), no & and no ;.
    A line is 128 characters, a command's argument tail 127.

EXAMPLES
    echo hello > /R1.TXT         a new file
    echo more >> /R1.TXT         added to it
    wc < /R1.TXT                 2 2 11
    cat /FRUIT.TXT | sort | uniq
    dir /BIN | grep VI
    sort < /FRUIT.TXT | uniq | wc > /COUNT.TXT
    cat /A.TXT /B.TXT > /AB.TXT  join two files
    cat /DOCS/OS.MD | more       page a pipe: keys from the keyboard
    man cat > /CAT.TXT           a page into a file, not paged

NOTES
    >> appends in place when the file is the last one written (its
    sectors end at the volume's free pointer), else it copies the old
    sectors to the free pointer first; the entry is rewritten only
    when the command is done, so a failed append leaves the old file.
    Programs built with y1cc --os send putchar/puts through the OS
    (the CONOUT syscall) and read with conin() (CONIN), which is what
    the shell redirects; keys come from keyin() (KEYIN).
    New in Y1/OS 2026-09-23 (the P8X shell had < > >> and a two-stage
    |; here the redirects apply to every command and a pipe has up to
    four stages).

